{"graph_chars": "New information technology hit two labor markets at once. Computers and networks were a substitute for low-skill file clerks. They were a complement for high-skill managers who could watch more information and juggle more work. Four steps, the same four from Chapter Three. One: draw the old markets. Two: the shock hits demand, not household supply. Three: low-skill demand shifts left. High-skill demand shifts right. Four: low-skill wage and quantity hired fall. High-skill wage and quantity hired rise. From the nineteen seventies to the mid two thousands, the wage gap widened. In nineteen eighty a college graduate earned about thirty percent more than a comparable high-school graduate. By two thousand nineteen, about fifty-nine percent more. Many economists blame improvements in technology.", "graph_times": [[0.0, 0.0643], [0.0643, 0.1285], [0.1285, 0.1928], [0.1928, 0.2571], [0.2571, 0.3213], [0.3213, 0.3856], [0.3856, 0.4498], [0.4498, 0.5141], [0.5141, 0.5784], [0.5784, 0.6426], [0.6426, 0.7069], [0.7069, 0.7712], [0.7712, 0.8354], [0.8354, 0.8997], [0.8997, 0.9639], [0.9639, 1.0282], [1.0282, 1.0925], [1.0925, 1.1567], [1.1567, 1.221], [1.221, 1.2853], [1.2853, 1.3495], [1.3495, 1.4138], [1.4138, 1.478], [1.478, 1.5423], [1.5423, 1.6066], [1.6066, 1.6708], [1.6708, 1.7351], [1.7351, 1.7994], [1.7994, 1.8636], [1.8636, 1.9279], [1.9279, 1.9921], [1.9921, 2.0564], [2.0564, 2.1207], [2.1207, 2.1849], [2.1849, 2.2492], [2.2492, 2.3135], [2.3135, 2.3777], [2.3777, 2.442], [2.442, 2.5062], [2.5062, 2.5705], [2.5705, 2.6348], [2.6348, 2.699], [2.699, 2.7633], [2.7633, 2.8276], [2.8276, 2.8918], [2.8918, 2.9561], [2.9561, 3.0203], [3.0203, 3.0846], [3.0846, 3.1489], [3.1489, 3.2131], [3.2131, 3.2774], [3.2774, 3.3417], [3.3417, 3.4059], [3.4059, 3.4702], [3.4702, 3.5344], [3.5344, 3.5987], [3.5987, 3.663], [3.663, 3.7272], [3.7272, 3.7915], [3.7915, 3.8558], [3.8558, 3.92], [3.92, 3.9843], [3.9843, 4.0485], [4.0485, 4.1128], [4.1128, 4.1771], [4.1771, 4.